---
/**
 * foodcraft / WhyBN — "WHY-US" (Panday Coffee Works / BN).
 *
 * ROLE: the argument block, drawn as a RUN OF RULED ENTRIES rather than a
 * headed section. It is the only section on this page with no heading of its
 * own: the rail says what it is, and beneath the rail the whole content cell is
 * nothing but full-width rows, each one a huge rust ORDINAL in a 140px column
 * beside an uppercase reason title and the owner's paragraph, ruled off with a
 * grey hairline. No eyebrow, no h2, no lead, no plate, no button — "Panday
 * Coffee Works Landing.dc.html" draws this section with the list and nothing
 * else, which is precisely why the rail label carries the section's name here
 * and does not elsewhere.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * that file's data-component="WHY-US". No literal hex is used as a VALUE
 * anywhere in this file (R1); the hexes below appear only inside this comment,
 * as the proof that each token named is the one holding the design's colour:
 *   section   border-bottom:1px #17181A  -> the wrapper's .sec (--rule-strong)
 *   rail      border-right:1px  #C8C7C2  -> the wrapper's .rail (--line)
 *   rail-t    10px mono /.3em   #A8351B  -> the wrapper's .rail-t (--accent)
 *   content cell   NO padding (see "TWO GEOMETRY DECISIONS" below)
 *   row       grid minmax(0,140px) minmax(0,1fr); gap 32px; padding 34px;
 *             border-bottom:1px #C8C7C2 -> --line; align-items:start
 *   numeral   800 / 52px / line-height .9 / -.04em / #A8351B -> --accent
 *   title h3  700 / 24px / -.02em / uppercase / margin 0 0 9px / #17181A -> --ink
 *   body p    15.5px / 1.8 / margin 0 / max-width 66ch / #3C3E42 -> --ink-2
 *
 * --ink-2 IS THE RIGHT TOKEN FOR THE PARAGRAPH, not --ink-soft. The design sets
 * body copy in #3C3E42, and --ink-2 is the token holding exactly that; --ink-soft
 * is #5A5C60, a different, lighter grey that this design uses for its mono
 * labels. #3C3E42 is darker than #5A5C60 on every channel, so the swap can only
 * raise contrast: COMPUTED on the native hexes (WCAG 2.x, sRGB relative
 * luminance), #3C3E42 on #EFEEEA is 9.23, against the 5.77 the wrapper's own
 * table verified for --ink-soft on the same ground. Re-run it if a hex moves.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX: `pcw-` (Panday Coffee Works / why). Deliberately NOT the BM
 * sibling's `tcw-` set and not BL's `why-*` set — an audit had to rename a whole
 * block of classes that arrived verbatim from the template before it. Only the
 * HOUSE STYLE is inherited from WhyBM (this doc block, the guard discipline, the
 * original-index-vs-display-index rule); the palette, the ruled-row layout, the
 * ordinal and every metric are this design's.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (both declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the "Why us" group, so the sidebar owns a real input for each — a
 * data-field on an undeclared path is a dead hook that focuses nothing):
 *   why.items.${i}.title   — the reason (h3)
 *   why.items.${i}.body    — the reason's paragraph. CANONICAL key: the value
 *                            can arrive as .description through astro-builder's
 *                            normalizeBlock alias map (line ~897 spells
 *                            { description: 'body' }) and as .desc from older
 *                            payloads, so the READ tolerates all three while the
 *                            data-field always spells the key the schema
 *                            declares — otherwise the sidebar never receives the
 *                            edit.
 * That is the complete list. This file writes no other data-field.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. Not one word of visible owner copy is
 * written here. The design's demo reasons are the DESIGNER filling in their own
 * template; a real roastery gets a page that says only what that roastery said.
 * Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an empty value removes the ELEMENT and
 * not just its text: no title over nothing, no empty paragraph, and — because a
 * whole blank row is dropped before render — never a stranded ordinal or a
 * hairline ruling off a reason that is not there.
 *
 * DERIVED, not authored:
 *   · THE RAIL LABEL, "03 / Why". Decorative page furniture, never an editable
 *     hook and never stored: 03 is this section's place in the RAILED sequence
 *     of PageBN's render order (Hero 00, About 01, Services 02, Why 03,
 *     Gallery 04 … — the header and the trust strip carry no rail and so take
 *     no index), which is the same number the design prints against this
 *     section. The WORD is the section's role, matching
 *     the wrapper's own generic naming, rather than the design's demo phrase
 *     "Why here" — the designer's words for their own shop are copy, and copy is
 *     the one thing this file may not author. The whole rail is aria-hidden: it
 *     is a rotated index mark, and a screen reader already has the document
 *     order it encodes (R9). It carries exactly one aria-hidden and no
 *     conditional one beside it.
 *   · THE ORDINAL. The design's demo data carries an `n` field; our schema
 *     declares no numeral input, so it is computed and zero-padded to two
 *     digits. It is the ONE thing keyed to the DISPLAY index rather than the
 *     original one (R6): after a blank row is dropped the printed sequence must
 *     read 01-02-03, never 01-03. Everything an EDIT touches uses the ORIGINAL
 *     index, so deleting reason 2 can never misdirect a write into reason 3.
 *     aria-hidden for the same reason as the rail.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED (so none of it reads as an oversight):
 *   · why.tag, why.headline, why.lead and why.image. All four are schema-declared
 *     and all four are drawn by sibling templates, but this design's WHY-US has
 *     no eyebrow, no headline, no lead paragraph and no plate — it is rail plus
 *     rows. Adding any of them would be building a different section than the
 *     one that was handed over. No plate also means no role="img" and no alt
 *     anywhere in this file (R9): there is no upload here to name.
 *   · NO STAR ROW (R2). This section carries no rating field and no review, and
 *     the design draws none here. Stars belong to TESTIMONIALS and only ever
 *     from a real numeric rating; five stars printed for a customer who gave no
 *     rating is a fabricated claim.
 *   · NO id ON THE SECTION. The page's ids are menu / about / gallery / visit /
 *     faq / main, and the design gives WHY-US none of them; derive-content-
 *     defaults emits a nav item pointing at '#why', which HeaderBN's ANCHOR_ALIAS
 *     remaps onto '#about'. An id here would create a target the navigation
 *     never points at and a second landing place for the same link.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR HELPER (R7), and this is a decision, not an omission.
 *     This section renders no href of any kind — no button, no link, no phone
 *     number — so there is nothing to remap and a copied helper would ship as
 *     dead code. If a link is ever added here, copy the helper from a BN sibling
 *     with PAGE_ANCHORS = menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main and
 *     ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping location,contact -> visit · services,rooms -> menu ·
 *     host,why -> about · work -> gallery · book -> main, passing tel: / mailto:
 *     / http through untouched.
 *   · NO ICON PER REASON. A drawn pictogram claims WHAT the reason is about; the
 *     owner's words are the only signal this section is allowed to carry. The
 *     ordinal claims nothing.
 *   · THE TITLE IS EMITTED AS TEXT, not set:html. The wrapper's second-voice
 *     emphasis — a COLOUR CHANGE, never an italic — is written for the headline
 *     (.h2 em / .em), and this section draws no headline, so a bare <em> typed
 *     into a list row would pick up nothing anyway. A list row is not a place to
 *     open markup. (Consequently no :global() rule is needed here: scoped styles
 *     cannot reach set:html children, and this file creates none — R3.)
 *
 * TWO GEOMETRY DECISIONS a reader would otherwise flag:
 *   · .sec-body IS NOT USED, and that is on purpose. The wrapper's content-cell
 *     primitive exists to pad the cell; this design's WHY-US cell is drawn with
 *     NO padding at all, because the 34px lives on each ROW so that every
 *     hairline runs the full width of the sheet edge to edge. Wearing .sec-body
 *     and then cancelling its only declaration would name a primitive for none
 *     of its effect. The cell keeps the primitive's other job — min-width:0 — as
 *     its own rule. .sec, .rail and .rail-t, the three that draw the SPINE, are
 *     used exactly as the wrapper defines them and nothing about them is
 *     restated here.
 *   · THE ROW'S HORIZONTAL PADDING IS THE WRAPPER'S CLAMP, not a flat 34px. The
 *     drawn 34px is kept as the CAP — clamp(20px,3vw,34px) resolves to exactly
 *     34px from ~1133px up, so at any desktop width this is the design's number.
 *     Below that it tracks .sec-body and the collapsed .rail, which use the same
 *     clamp, so this section's text edge stays flush with every neighbouring
 *     section's down the whole page. In a ruled sheet that shared left edge IS
 *     the design; a flat 34px here would sit 7px out of step with the sections
 *     above and below it at a 900px viewport (3vw = 27px there) and 11.2px out
 *     at 760px, where the rail collapses and takes the same clamp.
 *
 * THE LAST ROW KEEPS ITS HAIRLINE. The design's sc-for gives every row a
 * border-bottom, and the cell it repeats into has no padding, so in the handed-
 * over file the final grey hairline lands directly on the section's strong ink
 * rule and the sheet closes on a doubled edge. Reproduced as drawn: no
 * :last-child exception is written, because removing it would be re-authoring
 * the design's own boundary rather than fixing a bug.
 *
 * ROW COUNT: unbounded. The design hints three (hint-placeholder-count="3") but
 * these are stacked full-width rows, not fixed tracks — a fourth reason simply
 * adds a fourth ruled entry, so the proportions survive and no owner content is
 * refused. The reveal stagger tops out at the wrapper's last declared delay:
 * rows 1-4 take no delay, .07s, .14s and .21s, and every row from the FIFTH on
 * reveals together with the fourth at .21s.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBN passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;


const why = content.why || {};

type Item = { title?: string; body?: string; desc?: string; description?: string };
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(why.items) ? why.items : [];
const titleOf = (it: Item): string => String(it?.title ?? '').trim();
const bodyOf = (it: Item): string => String(it?.body ?? it?.desc ?? it?.description ?? '').trim();

// A reason the owner added but never filled would otherwise spend a 52px
// ordinal, 34px of padding and a hairline on nothing. Drop the blanks
// (whitespace counts as blank) but carry the ORIGINAL index `i` so every
// data-field still writes into the real array slot.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ i, title: titleOf(it), body: bodyOf(it) }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.body));

// SECTION GATE (R5). Counts the ROWS only — the real body content. There is no
// tag or headline in this section to be tempted into gating on, but the reason
// holds anyway: lib/derive-content-defaults.ts emits why.tag ('Why us') and
// why.headline ('Why choose X') for every site while shipping items: [], so any
// gate touching those would be always-true and would ship an empty ruled cell
// under a rail on every first build.
const hasReasons = rows.length > 0;
---
{hasReasons && (
  <section class="sec">
    {/* The SPINE, exactly as the wrapper defines it. aria-hidden because the
        rotated index is decoration; see the frontmatter. */}
    <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rail-t">Why</span></div>
    <div class="pcw-list">
      {rows.map((r, d: number) => (
        /* `d` is the DISPLAY index and `r.i` is the ORIGINAL one. The ordinal is
           the only thing keyed to `d`, so the printed sequence stays unbroken
           after a blank row is dropped. */
        <div class="pcw-row reveal" data-delay={d > 0 ? String(Math.min(d, 3)) : undefined}>
          <div class="pcw-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(d + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
          <div class="pcw-c">
            {r.title && <h3 class="pcw-t" data-field={`why.items.${r.i}.title`}>{r.title}</h3>}
            {r.body && <p class="pcw-p" data-field={`why.items.${r.i}.body`}>{r.body}</p>}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* ── THE CONTENT CELL. Unpadded on purpose — the 34px belongs to each row so
        that every hairline runs edge to edge (see the frontmatter for why
        .sec-body is not worn here). min-width:0 is the one thing kept from that
        primitive: a grid item's default min-width is auto, so one unbroken word
        in a reason would otherwise widen this cell and push the whole page
        sideways (R8). */
  .pcw-list { min-width: 0; }

  /* ── THE RULED ENTRY. Tracks, gap, padding and rule exactly as drawn; the
        horizontal padding is the wrapper's own clamp so this section's text edge
        stays flush with its neighbours' (frontmatter). align-items:start keeps
        the ordinal's cap-line level with the title instead of centring it
        against a paragraph of unknown length. */
  .pcw-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 140px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 32px;
    align-items: start;
    padding: 34px clamp(20px, 3vw, 34px);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* ── THE ORDINAL. Archivo 800 at 52px, NOT the wrapper's .fig primitive: .fig
        is a MONO figure (var(--mono), weight 500) and this numeral is display
        type — same voice, weight and negative tracking as the page's headlines,
        which is why it takes var(--disp) and follows it under any font pairing.
        Lining tabular figures are the only property here the design does not
        spell out; they change no metric it states and simply stop 1s and 0s
        setting to different widths down a column of ordinals. */
  .pcw-n {
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 52px;
    line-height: .9;
    letter-spacing: -.04em;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .pcw-c { min-width: 0; }

  /* ── THE REASON. The wrapper's shared h1-h4 rule already carries var(--disp),
        --ink and the uppercase; only what this design draws DIFFERENTLY is
        restated — 700 rather than 800, -.02em rather than -.03em, the 9px below,
        and the LEADING. The design writes no line-height on this h3 and its
        stylesheet sets none, so `normal` is literally what it draws; the shared
        rule is .98, which is the headline's display leading and would set a 24px
        uppercase title tighter than it was drawn the moment an owner's reason
        runs to two lines. Guessing a number instead would be a divergence of its
        own. */
  .pcw-t {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 24px;
    letter-spacing: -.02em;
    line-height: normal;
    margin: 0 0 9px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Qualified with its parent so it clears the wrapper's `:where(p){margin:0 0
     1em}`: the design's paragraph has no bottom margin at all, and the 66ch cap
     is the measure it is drawn to. A max-width is a ceiling and never a floor —
     on a track narrower than the measure the paragraph simply fills the row —
     so it cannot push anything sideways at any width. */
  .pcw-c .pcw-p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── 900 · the entry stops being two columns. The ordinal moves above its
        reason rather than beside it, which is also what keeps a 140px fixed-ish
        column from eating a third of a phone. Every step from here down only
        ever DECREASES as the viewport narrows — 52 -> 42 -> 36, 24 -> 21 -> 19,
        34 -> 28 -> 24 — and no size in this file is a clamp except the shared
        horizontal padding, whose floor (20px) sits below its cap (34px). There
        is no floor anywhere here that a narrower query's cap can fall beneath,
        so the ladder is monotonic by construction (R8).
        Longhand padding, so the horizontal clamp above survives untouched and
        this section never falls out of line with its neighbours. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .pcw-row {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 12px;
      padding-top: 28px;
      padding-bottom: 28px;
    }
    .pcw-n { font-size: 42px; }
    .pcw-t { font-size: 21px; }
  }

  /* ── 520 · tighten the stack. Nothing here may push the page sideways at
        360px: both owner strings break anywhere, every track is minmax(0,…) or
        min-width:0, and there is no button in this section to give
        white-space:normal to. The paragraph keeps its drawn 15.5px at every
        width — it is already a comfortable phone size, and shrinking it would be
        the one unforced size change in the file. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .pcw-row {
      gap: 10px;
      padding-top: 24px;
      padding-bottom: 24px;
    }
    .pcw-n { font-size: 36px; }
    .pcw-t { font-size: 19px; }
  }
</style>
